#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime } &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\ny\\frac{dy}{dx} &= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\int ydy &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C \\\\\ny^{2} &= \\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C \\\\\ny &= \\pm  \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C} \\\\\ny &= \\pm  \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + K}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime } + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2xy \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int (-2x)dx \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y}dy &= -2\\int xdx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x)$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2} + C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2}} \\cdot e^{C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= Ke^{-x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$ and $K > 0$.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2},\\qquad K\\ne0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0$, separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is separable when it has the form $y'=h(x)g(y)$. Here, since $x,y\\ge0$,\n$$\\sqrt{2xy}=\\sqrt{2x}\\sqrt y.$$\nWhere $y>0$, division by $\\sqrt y$ gives the separated form\n$$\\frac1{\\sqrt y}\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\sqrt{2x}.$$\nThe division does not apply at $y=0$. The original equation also admits the equilibrium solution $y=0$, which must be considered separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{x}{y}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x}{y} \\\\\ny\\frac{dy}{dx} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\int ydy &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + K \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + 2K \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aed1cbca-9e55-5892-af1d-df1ff1509101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arctan y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065819} {:answer/id #uuid "fef1e70c-da45-5a86-85ab-5b4aac412df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065820} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8469a6-2ebc-5595-a41a-d5fd323f541a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065821} {:answer/id #uuid "494bc739-0e89-510d-aed4-e0553dd43d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arccos y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065822} {:answer/id #uuid "5291991b-f6ee-55f2-8bdd-941a8a475bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arcsin y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5291991b-f6ee-55f2-8bdd-941a8a475bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arcsin y = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065823} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdbc7d89-cef8-5720-be51-44d59b581954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc5a6535-55d7-58bf-9da3-473b544214d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-154367" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}$ for $\\mid y \\mid < 1$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The condition $|y|<1$ permits division by $\\sqrt{1-y^2}$:\n$$\\frac{dy}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}=dx.$$\nIntegrating gives $\\arcsin y=x+C$. An explicit form is $y=\\sin(x+C)$ on intervals where $-\\pi/2<x+C<\\pi/2$, which preserves $y'=\\sqrt{1-y^2}>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9372fe8a-9fe7-5b4d-bda4-372a083ddca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065831} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f4abee-366d-5610-8fb5-d09a162b2c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y^{2}}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065832} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f10af7-a270-5ce4-a424-4307343938cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1) \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = xy^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065833} {:answer/id #uuid "714ca07c-83ac-5ede-be6f-c4ae92b77715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065834} {:answer/id #uuid "75b0eb6d-0154-58d2-9a2d-8276d5ffdc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b0eb6d-0154-58d2-9a2d-8276d5ffdc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065835} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e4bc99-ff97-5d84-85c3-18160f4ad8ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8143064d-a592-5c44-b653-c218df46c5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-154678" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x + 1}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Factor the right-hand side as $\\frac{x}{x+1}y^2$. Where $y\\ne0$, divide both sides by $y^2$:\n$$\\frac1{y^2}\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{x+1}.$$\nThis is the separated form for $x\\ne-1$. The solution $y=0$ must be considered separately because division by $y^2$ excludes it."}]}
